WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.275
Lesson 2.5.

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.028
MEV and protecting your trades.

00:00:05.028 --> 00:00:07.403
By the end, you'll be able to set slippage

00:00:07.403 --> 00:00:09.998
and routing so your trades aren't easy targets.

00:00:11.298 --> 00:00:12.588
Here's the plan.

00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:15.121
First, what M.E.V. actually is, and the

00:00:15.121 --> 00:00:18.017
specific attack that targets your trades.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.404
Second, the real defences, with a worked

00:00:20.404 --> 00:00:23.207
example showing exactly what they're worth.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:24.804
And third, the checklist to

00:00:24.804 --> 00:00:26.287
run before any large swap.

00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:31.027
Your slippage tolerance is not just a safety setting.

00:00:31.027 --> 00:00:33.710
On a pending trade, it's also the profit ceiling

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.447
for whoever is watching that trade in the mempool.

00:00:37.747 --> 00:00:39.872
What MEV actually is.

00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:45.796
M.E.V., maximal extractable value, is profit taken by whoever

00:00:45.796 --> 00:00:50.197
controls the order transactions land in inside a block.

00:00:50.197 --> 00:00:52.437
Some of it is harmless, arbitrage

00:00:52.437 --> 00:00:55.505
that simply realigns prices across venues.

00:00:55.505 --> 00:00:58.270
The kind that hurts you is a sandwich attack.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.312
A bot sees your pending swap, buys

00:01:00.312 --> 00:01:02.830
just before you, pushing the price up.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.787
It lets your trade execute at that

00:01:04.787 --> 00:01:06.800
worse price, then sells right after.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:11.006
Watch a sandwich attack happen, step by step.

00:01:11.006 --> 00:01:13.109
A bot watches the public mempool and

00:01:13.109 --> 00:01:15.966
sees your pending swap before it's confirmed.

00:01:15.966 --> 00:01:17.474
It buys first, in front of

00:01:17.474 --> 00:01:19.226
you, pushing the price up.

00:01:19.226 --> 00:01:22.388
Your trade executes right after, at that now-worse

00:01:22.388 --> 00:01:25.911
price, exactly what your slippage tolerance allowed.

00:01:25.911 --> 00:01:27.462
And the bot sells immediately

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.624
after you, pocketing the difference.

00:01:29.624 --> 00:01:31.498
Your slippage tolerance was its

00:01:31.498 --> 00:01:33.314
profit ceiling the entire time.

00:01:34.614 --> 00:01:35.704
Quick check.

00:01:35.704 --> 00:01:37.915
What actually limits how much a sandwich

00:01:37.915 --> 00:01:39.479
bot can take from your trade?

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:46.974
The answer: your slippage tolerance, and the pool's depth.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.429
Real defences.

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:52.609
Here are the real defences.

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:55.907
Set a tight but realistic slippage, zero point

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:58.855
one to zero point five percent for liquid

00:01:58.855 --> 00:02:02.734
majors, wider only when you understand exactly why.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:06.563
Use M.E.V.-protected or private transaction routing,

00:02:06.563 --> 00:02:09.609
many wallets and aggregators offer it.

00:02:09.609 --> 00:02:13.629
Split large trades instead of broadcasting one huge swap.

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:16.936
And avoid thin pools entirely for size that matters.

00:02:18.236 --> 00:02:21.311
Here's exactly where those dollar figures come from.

00:02:21.311 --> 00:02:23.856
Swap size, twenty thousand dollars.

00:02:23.856 --> 00:02:27.103
At three percent slippage tolerance, the maximum

00:02:27.103 --> 00:02:29.687
value at risk is twenty thousand times

00:02:29.687 --> 00:02:32.836
zero point zero three, six hundred dollars.

00:02:32.836 --> 00:02:35.582
Tighten that to zero point five percent,

00:02:35.582 --> 00:02:38.395
and it's twenty thousand times zero point

00:02:38.395 --> 00:02:41.106
zero zero five, one hundred dollars.

00:02:41.106 --> 00:02:44.086
Your slippage percentage, times your trade size,

00:02:44.086 --> 00:02:47.066
is quite literally the sandwich's profit ceiling.

00:02:48.366 --> 00:02:50.121
Swapping $20,000: at

00:02:50.121 --> 00:02:54.168
3% slippage tolerance, a sandwich can take up to

00:02:54.168 --> 00:02:57.936
around $600. Tighten that to 0.5%, and its

00:02:57.936 --> 00:03:00.530
ceiling drops to around $100. Route

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.569
it privately or protected instead, and

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:08.167
the transaction is never visible in the public mempool

00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:11.929
at all, so it can't be sandwiched from there.

00:03:13.229 --> 00:03:14.319
Quick check.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.038
Why not just set slippage as

00:03:16.038 --> 00:03:17.579
close to zero as possible?

00:03:21.999 --> 00:03:25.025
The answer: normal, ordinary price movement will

00:03:25.025 --> 00:03:27.618
make the transaction revert, and a failed

00:03:27.618 --> 00:03:29.594
transaction still costs you gas.

00:03:30.894 --> 00:03:34.073
Too tight a tolerance, say 0.05% on a

00:03:34.073 --> 00:03:38.304
volatile pair, just makes the transaction fail.

00:03:38.304 --> 00:03:41.694
And a failed transaction still costs gas, exactly

00:03:41.694 --> 00:03:44.474
what Lesson 1.2 already warned you about.

00:03:45.774 --> 00:03:47.499
Last check for this section.

00:03:47.499 --> 00:03:50.144
Name two real M.E.V. defences.

00:03:54.444 --> 00:03:56.699
The answer: any two of these, tight

00:03:56.699 --> 00:04:00.206
slippage, private or protected routing, splitting large

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:01.959
trades, or using deep pools.

00:04:03.259 --> 00:04:04.924
Where this protection breaks down.

00:04:06.224 --> 00:04:10.179
Here's how people undo these defences without realizing it.

00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:12.823
Using the exact same slippage setting for

00:04:12.823 --> 00:04:15.529
every trade, regardless of how volatile or

00:04:15.529 --> 00:04:18.095
thin that specific pair actually is.

00:04:18.095 --> 00:04:22.110
Assuming an app's protected-routing feature is automatically on, when

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.105
it often has to be switched on per transaction.

00:04:25.105 --> 00:04:29.572
Treating protected routing as total M.E.V. immunity, when other

00:04:29.572 --> 00:04:34.270
extraction methods beyond the classic sandwich can still exist.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.265
And ignoring pool depth entirely, watching only

00:04:37.265 --> 00:04:39.887
the slippage percentage, when a thin pool

00:04:39.887 --> 00:04:42.570
makes any slippage setting less protective.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.235
Before any large swap.

00:04:46.535 --> 00:04:48.730
Run this before any large swap.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.478
Slippage set specifically for this pair and this

00:04:51.478 --> 00:04:54.190
size, never left sitting at a high default.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:57.510
Protected routing switched on for anything sizeable.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:59.472
And large orders either split, or

00:04:59.472 --> 00:05:01.780
routed through genuinely deep liquidity.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.835
Your turn, 5 minutes.

00:05:04.835 --> 00:05:07.918
Open your wallet or aggregator's slippage setting right

00:05:07.918 --> 00:05:10.475
now, and check what it's actually set to.

00:05:10.475 --> 00:05:12.327
If it's sitting at a high default,

00:05:12.327 --> 00:05:14.180
fix it before your next real trade.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.521
To recap: MEV is profit from controlling transaction order,

00:05:19.521 --> 00:05:23.765
and a sandwich attack specifically targets your pending trade,

00:05:23.765 --> 00:05:27.365
using your own slippage tolerance as its ceiling.

00:05:27.365 --> 00:05:30.710
Tight but realistic slippage, protected routing, and

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.355
splitting large trades are real, measurable defences.

00:05:34.355 --> 00:05:36.561
And too tight a tolerance just fails

00:05:36.561 --> 00:05:39.245
the transaction, costing you gas for nothing.

00:05:40.545 --> 00:05:43.475
That's MEV and protecting your trades.

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:47.306
Next up, Lesson 2.6: advanced execution,

00:05:47.306 --> 00:05:50.670
limit, TWAP and intent-based orders.
